{"teams":[{"idTeam":"140456","idESPN":"0","idAPIfootball":"15340","intLoved":null,"strTeam":"Anaitasuna","strTeamAlternate":"Club Deportivo Anaitasuna","strTeamShort":"","intFormedYear":"1934","strSport":"Soccer","strLeague":"Copa del Rey","idLeague":"4483","strLeague2":"","idLeague2":null,"strLeague3":"","idLeague3":null,"strLeague4":"","idLeague4":null,"strLeague5":"","idLeague5":null,"strLeague6":"","idLeague6":null,"strLeague7":"","idLeague7":null,"strDivision":null,"idVenue":"20233","strStadium":"Campo Txerloia","strKeywords":"","strRSS":"","strLocation":"Azkoitia, Basque Country, Spain","strWebsite":"","strFacebook":"","strTwitter":"","strInstagram":"","strDescriptionEN":"Club Deportivo Anaitasuna is a Spanish football team based in Azkoitia, in the Autonomous Community of the Basque Country. Founded in 1934 it plays in the Tercera División (Group 4), holding home games at Campo Txerloia which has a capacity of 1,500 spectators. The team's name in Basque means brotherhood. Their closest rivals are Lagun Onak from Azpeitia.\r\n\r\nHaving won the Campeonato de Aficionados (national amateur cup) in 1973, their most successful period was in the 1980s when they were members of the Tercera División across the decade. In 1980–81 and 1982–83, their 6th-place league finish earned entry to the following season's Copa del Rey. Since then they have mostly played at provincial level, being promoted as the Gipuzkoa league winners four times (1993, 2001, 2012, 2016) but being relegated back down immediately on each occasion. They went up again in 2020.\r\n\r\nThe club has youth sections and a women's team (who finished 6th in Spain in 1994–95), and also competes in several other sports.","strDescriptionDE":null,"strDescriptionFR":null,"strDescriptionCN":null,"strDescriptionIT":null,"strDescriptionJP":null,"strDescriptionRU":null,"strDescriptionES":null,"strDescriptionPT":null,"strDescriptionSE":null,"strDescriptionNL":null,"strDescriptionHU":null,"strDescriptionNO":null,"str